### Meshtastic Kafka - REST Client scratch file (VS Code humao.rest-client)
# Signup starts unverified - run "Create owner", then "Verify email" with the
# token from mailhog/docker logs. See docs/architecture.md#registry-email-verification.

@registryUrl = http://localhost:8090
@readingsUrl = http://localhost:8091

@ownerName = Oscar
@ownerEmail = oscar@example.com
@apiKey = paste-api-key-or-docker-logs-here
@superuserApiKey = paste-superuser-api-key-here
@verificationToken = paste-token-from-mailhog-or-docker-logs-here
@resetToken = paste-password-reset-token-from-mailhog-here

@meshChannelId = TestChan
@meshPsk = AAAA
@nodeId = 111222333
@environmentVariantId = paste-telemetry-variant-id-here
@airQualityVariantId = paste-telemetry-variant-id-here


### ================= Health =================

### registry health
GET {{registryUrl}}/health

### readings health
GET {{readingsUrl}}/health


### ================= Owners =================

### Account terms - signing up accepts them, so read them first (no auth)
# @name accountTerms
GET {{registryUrl}}/terms?locale=en

### Create owner (signup) - starts unverified, api_key returned exactly once
# @name signup
POST {{registryUrl}}/owners
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "{{ownerName}}",
  "email": "{{ownerEmail}}",
  "password": "Correct-Horse-Battery-42",
  "terms_version": "{{accountTerms.response.body.$.version}}",
  "terms_locale": "en"
}

### Accept changed account terms - they never lapse, so this pauses or resumes nothing
POST {{registryUrl}}/owners/me/terms-acceptance
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "terms_version": "{{accountTerms.response.body.$.version}}"
}

### Resend verification email (3/hour per owner) - useful if the first one didn't arrive
POST {{registryUrl}}/owners/me/resend-verification
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Verify email - paste the token from the email into @verificationToken above first
GET {{registryUrl}}/owners/verify-email?token={{verificationToken}}

### Log in with a password - sets a session cookie, also for an unverified owner
### (is_active false). 10 attempts a minute per email and source IP, then 429.
POST {{registryUrl}}/owners/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{ownerEmail}}",
  "password": "Correct-Horse-Battery-42"
}

### Request a password reset link (always 200)
POST {{registryUrl}}/owners/request-password-reset
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{ownerEmail}}"
}

### Reset the password - paste the token from the email into @resetToken above first
POST {{registryUrl}}/owners/reset-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "token": "{{resetToken}}",
  "new_password": "Correct-Horse-Battery-42"
}

### List owners (public directory, no auth, no email)
GET {{registryUrl}}/owners

### Get one owner by id (public, no email)
GET {{registryUrl}}/owners/{{signup.response.body.$.id}}

### My own record on the public detail route - full, because it is me
### email, is_staff, is_active, email_verified_at, email_verification_sent_at, last_login
GET {{registryUrl}}/owners/{{signup.response.body.$.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### List owners as a superuser - the full record for every owner
### A plain owner key still gets the public shape here, on purpose, so that every
### row in the array has the same shape. Use the detail route above for your own.
GET {{registryUrl}}/owners
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Get my own profile - the same full record, plus the devices I may access
GET {{registryUrl}}/owners/me
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Update my profile - changing email resets verification and sends a new link
PUT {{registryUrl}}/owners/me
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "name": "Test Owner Renamed"
}

### Opt in to emails about my sources and sinks (both off by default; superusers always get busy-source emails)
PUT {{registryUrl}}/owners/me
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "status_alerts_enabled": true,
  "source_rate_alerts_enabled": true
}

### Rotate my API key - invalidates the old one immediately
POST {{registryUrl}}/owners/me/rotate-key
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Device IDs I can see (used by readings to scope telemetry access)
GET {{registryUrl}}/owners/me/devices
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Delete my own account (cascades to meshes/devices) - careful, destructive
# DELETE {{registryUrl}}/owners/me
# Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}


### ================= Meshes =================
# Requires a verified owner - run "Verify email" above first.

### Mesh terms - a mesh on the default broker needs them accepted
# @name meshTerms
GET {{registryUrl}}/meshes/terms?locale=en
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Create a mesh
# @name createMesh
POST {{registryUrl}}/meshes
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "channel_id": "{{meshChannelId}}",
  "psk_b64": "{{meshPsk}}",
  "label": "Test mesh",
  "terms_version": "{{meshTerms.response.body.$.version}}",
  "terms_locale": "en"
}

### Renew the mesh terms - resumes a paused mesh and cancels its deletion
POST {{registryUrl}}/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}/terms-acceptance
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "terms_version": "{{meshTerms.response.body.$.version}}"
}

### List my meshes
GET {{registryUrl}}/meshes
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Get one mesh
GET {{registryUrl}}/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Rotate the mesh's PSK - republishes every registered gateway on it
PUT {{registryUrl}}/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "psk_b64": "BBBB"
}

### List devices registered or sighted on this mesh (including its gateway(s))
GET {{registryUrl}}/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}/devices
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Make the mesh public - readable by anyone, writes stay with the owner
PUT {{registryUrl}}/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "is_public": true
}

### Public meshes, no credentials - public fields only (no psk_b64, source_id or terms)
GET {{registryUrl}}/meshes

### One public mesh and its allowed devices, no credentials
GET {{registryUrl}}/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}/devices

### Delete the mesh (cascades to every device on it) - careful, destructive
# DELETE {{registryUrl}}/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}
# Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}


### ================= Sources (remote MQTT brokers) =================
# Every source route needs an operator key, and an operator only sees the sources
# they created. A superuser grants the role with "Make an owner an operator" below.
# Topics blocked by REGISTRY__SOURCES_BLOCKED_TOPICS (# by default) answer 400.

### Ask to become an operator - every superuser is emailed
# @name operatorRequest
POST {{registryUrl}}/owners/me/operator-request
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "reason": "I run the regional broker for our community"
}

### My latest operator request
GET {{registryUrl}}/owners/me/operator-request
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Pending operator requests (superuser key)
GET {{registryUrl}}/operator-requests
Authorization: Bearer {{superuserApiKey}}

### Approve the request (superuser key) - or "reject"
PUT {{registryUrl}}/operator-requests/{{operatorRequest.response.body.$.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{superuserApiKey}}

{
  "decision": "approve",
  "note": "Welcome aboard"
}

### Or make an owner an operator directly (superuser key)
PUT {{registryUrl}}/owners/{{signup.response.body.$.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{superuserApiKey}}

{
  "role": "operator"
}

### The source terms - every statement must be accepted before creating a source
# @name sourceTerms
GET {{registryUrl}}/sources/terms?locale=en
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Create a source - bridges the remote topic into this instance
# @name createSource
POST {{registryUrl}}/sources
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "label": "public meshtastic, EU",
  "server": "mqtt.meshtastic.org:1883",
  "ssl": false,
  "username": "meshdev",
  "password": "large4cats",
  "topic": "msh/EU_868/2/e/{{meshChannelId}}/#",
  "terms_version": "{{sourceTerms.response.body.$.version}}",
  "terms_locale": "en"
}

### Renew the terms - restarts a paused source and cancels its meshes' deletion
POST {{registryUrl}}/sources/{{createSource.response.body.$.id}}/terms-acceptance
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "terms_version": "{{sourceTerms.response.body.$.version}}"
}

### Scheduled deletions of stored readings (superuser key)
GET {{registryUrl}}/data-deletions?status=all
Authorization: Bearer {{superuserApiKey}}

### List sources
GET {{registryUrl}}/sources
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Get one source, with the live status of its bridge
GET {{registryUrl}}/sources/{{createSource.response.body.$.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Restart a source that reads connected but has stopped receiving - re-applies and resubscribes; 409 if paused
POST {{registryUrl}}/sources/{{createSource.response.body.$.id}}/restart
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Attach the mesh to the source - its gateways are then only accepted through it
PUT {{registryUrl}}/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "source_id": "{{createSource.response.body.$.id}}"
}

### Meshes on the source
GET {{registryUrl}}/meshes?source_id={{createSource.response.body.$.id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Detach the mesh again, back to this instance's own topic
PUT {{registryUrl}}/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "source_id": null
}

### Delete the source - 409 while meshes are attached
# DELETE {{registryUrl}}/sources/{{createSource.response.body.$.id}}
# Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}


### ================= Devices: gateways =================
# device_id is unique only while ALLOWED - see the takeover section below. Only the
# mesh owner can register a gateway.

### Register a gateway on the mesh above - this is what actually starts decoding
# @name createGateway
POST {{registryUrl}}/devices
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "node_id": {{nodeId}},
  "mesh_id": "{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}",
  "is_gateway": true,
  "label": "office gateway"
}

### List my gateway devices (both allowed and rejected)
GET {{registryUrl}}/devices?is_gateway=true
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### List gateway devices filtered by mesh and/or allow status
GET {{registryUrl}}/devices?mesh_id={{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}&is_gateway=true&is_allowed=true
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### List devices by the telemetry variants they reported (ids from GET /telemetry-variants)
### Operators: has_any_of, has_all_of, has_none_of; bare ids mean has_any_of.
### Each device record also carries telemetry_variant_ids for client-side filtering.
GET {{registryUrl}}/devices?telemetry_variant=has_all_of:{{environmentVariantId}}|{{airQualityVariantId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Same filter on one mesh's devices
GET {{registryUrl}}/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}/devices?telemetry_variant=has_none_of:{{environmentVariantId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Get one device
GET {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createGateway.response.body.$.uuid}}
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Rename a gateway's label (device_id/mesh/role are fixed at creation)
PUT {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createGateway.response.body.$.uuid}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "label": "renamed gateway"
}

### --- Takeover flow: move a gateway to a different mesh without losing history ---

### Step 1: reject the gateway - stops decoding, keeps the row
PUT {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createGateway.response.body.$.uuid}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "is_allowed": false
}

### Step 2: create a second mesh to take the device over to
# @name createMeshB
POST {{registryUrl}}/meshes
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "channel_id": "TakeoverChan",
  "psk_b64": "CCCC",
  "terms_version": "{{meshTerms.response.body.$.version}}"
}

### Step 3: register the same device_id on the new mesh - now succeeds (409 while allowed)
POST {{registryUrl}}/devices
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "node_id": {{nodeId}},
  "mesh_id": "{{createMeshB.response.body.$.id}}",
  "is_gateway": true,
  "label": "moved gateway"
}

### Delete a gateway - careful, destructive
# DELETE {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createGateway.response.body.$.uuid}}
# Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}


### ================= Devices: nodes (admin keys) =================
# Normally auto-discovered on first decode - see
# docs/architecture.md#device-registration-and-the-is-allowed-gate.

### Pre-register a node's admin keys on the mesh above
# @name createNode
POST {{registryUrl}}/devices
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "node_id": {{nodeId}},
  "mesh_id": "{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}",
  "is_gateway": false,
  "admin_keys_b64": ["AAAA"],
  "label": "test node"
}

### List my node devices
GET {{registryUrl}}/devices?is_gateway=false
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Get one node device
GET {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Update a node's admin keys (replaces the whole list) or label
PUT {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "label": "renamed node"
}

### Reject a node - mesh owner's lever to stop its data being stored; decode_job.py
### stops even attempting to decrypt its future packets
PUT {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "is_allowed": false
}

### Delete a node - careful, destructive
# DELETE {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}
# Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}


### ================= Measurement type / telemetry variant catalog (read-only) =================
# GET is open to any verified owner; POST/PUT/DELETE need a superuser key - see
# api-authenticated.http for the write examples. Rows are pre-seeded from the
# protobuf schema when the registry_api container starts, not created via this
# API. TelemetryVariant is keyed by wire-format message type (e.g.
# "telemetry:environment_metrics"), NOT verified hardware - see
# docs/architecture.md#sensor--measurement-catalog.

### List measurement types (unit/quantity_kind/vocabulary_uri per field)
GET {{registryUrl}}/measurements
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Filter: ?kind= (namespace before the colon), ?payload_kind= (exact), ?field_name=
GET {{registryUrl}}/measurements?kind=computed
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

###
GET {{registryUrl}}/measurements?payload_kind=telemetry:environment_metrics
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Which telemetry types a device reports, and which channels it produces.
### Two endpoints, not one - the old /devices/{id}/sensors nested the second
### inside the first. See docs/architecture.md#sensor--measurement-catalog.
GET {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}/telemetry-variants
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

###
GET {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}/measurements
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### List telemetry variants (default_reading_interval_seconds + optional curated hardware hint)
# @name listTelemetryVariants
GET {{registryUrl}}/telemetry-variants
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### List the fields a telemetry variant carries, and their detector defaults
GET {{registryUrl}}/telemetry-variants/{{listTelemetryVariants.response.body.$[?(@.payload_kind=='telemetry:environment_metrics')].id}}/measurements
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}


### ================= Postprocessing =================
# A blueprint is a reusable recipe: input slots, and ordered steps each running
# an algorithm. Attaching it to a device binds the slots to that device's
# channels and gives it the device's own calibration. See
# docs/architecture.md#postprocessing and docs/data-quality.md#postprocessing.

### What algorithms are available, and which params are per-device calibration
GET {{registryUrl}}/algorithms
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Create a blueprint - step "corrected" reads step "dew"'s output
# @name createBlueprint
POST {{registryUrl}}/postprocessing-blueprints
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "name": "weather-derived",
  "description": "dew point, then temperature-corrected",
  "inputs": [
    {"slot": "temperature", "default_channel": "environment_metrics.temperature"},
    {"slot": "humidity", "default_channel": "environment_metrics.relative_humidity"}
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "key": "dew",
      "order": 0,
      "function_name": "dew_point",
      "inputs": {"temperature": "slot:temperature", "relative_humidity": "slot:humidity"},
      "outputs": {"dew_point": "dew_point"}
    },
    {
      "key": "corrected",
      "order": 1,
      "function_name": "linear_temperature_correction",
      "inputs": {"value": "step:dew.dew_point", "temperature": "slot:temperature"},
      "params": {"reference_temperature": 20.0},
      "outputs": {"corrected": "dew_point_corrected"}
    }
  ]
}

### List my blueprints and public ones
GET {{registryUrl}}/postprocessing-blueprints
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Steps are validated when written -> 400 (calibration belongs to the device)
POST {{registryUrl}}/postprocessing-blueprints
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "name": "broken",
  "inputs": [{"slot": "value"}],
  "steps": [
    {"key": "cal", "order": 0, "function_name": "offset_scale",
     "inputs": {"value": "slot:value"}, "params": {"offset": 1.0}, "outputs": {"calibrated": "x"}}
  ]
}

### Attach it to a device. Slots bind to the default channels the device has;
### `problems` lists what still keeps it from being enabled.
# @name attachBlueprint
POST {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}/postprocessings
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{"blueprint_id": "{{createBlueprint.response.body.$.id}}"}

### Calibrate this device's sensor, in force from valid_from onwards
POST {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}/postprocessings/{{attachBlueprint.response.body.$.id}}/calibrations
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{"step_key": "corrected", "params": {"coefficient": 0.002}, "valid_from": "2026-09-01T00:00:00Z"}

### Enable it - refused while any slot is unbound or a step lacks its calibration
PUT {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}/postprocessings/{{attachBlueprint.response.body.$.id}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{"is_enabled": true}

### Recompute a week of stored history (stored like live values, never alarmed on)
POST {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}/postprocessings/{{attachBlueprint.response.body.$.id}}/backfills
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{"start": "2026-09-01T00:00:00Z", "stop": "2026-09-08T00:00:00Z"}

### Backfill progress: pending -> running -> done/failed
GET {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}/postprocessings/{{attachBlueprint.response.body.$.id}}/backfills
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Devices with a postprocessing attached
GET {{registryUrl}}/devices?has_postprocessing=true
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Both fault-detection intervals. reading vs publish are deliberately
### different: "not reading" and "not publishing" are separate faults once
### telemetry is batched - see docs/architecture.md#device-fault-detection.
PUT {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{"reading_interval_seconds": 300, "publish_interval_seconds": 3600}

### Clear both intervals - stops absence monitoring for this device
PUT {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "reading_interval_seconds": null,
  "publish_interval_seconds": null
}


### ================= Error-path sanity checks =================

### Duplicate email on signup -> 409
POST {{registryUrl}}/owners
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Duplicate",
  "email": "{{ownerEmail}}",
  "password": "Correct-Horse-Battery-42",
  "terms_version": "{{accountTerms.response.body.$.version}}"
}

### No auth on a protected route -> 401
GET {{registryUrl}}/meshes

### Invalid/garbage verification token -> 400
GET {{registryUrl}}/owners/verify-email?token=not-a-real-token

### Unverified owner hitting a gated route -> 403 (run this right after "Create owner",
### before "Verify email")
POST {{registryUrl}}/meshes
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

{
  "channel_id": "ShouldFail",
  "psk_b64": "AAAA"
}


### ================= Readings role =================
# Reuses the same bearer key as registry (HTTP introspection) - see
# docs/architecture.md#readings-authentication.

### Two ways to address the same device, both landing on the same view and
### returning identical bodies. See docs/api-reference.md#which-address-to-use.
###
### 1. By registry device UUID - the default, and the same identifier
###    GET {{registryUrl}}/devices/{{id}} takes. The "Pre-register a node"
###    request above (@name createNode) creates this exact device, so both
###    forms below address the same one.
GET {{readingsUrl}}/readings/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}/latest?lookback=-30d
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### 2. By mesh + Meshtastic node number - the store-native address, and the
###    only one that reaches a device with telemetry but no registration row.
###    "nodes", not "devices": a different identifier space from the UUID above.
GET {{readingsUrl}}/readings/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}/nodes/{{nodeId}}/latest?lookback=-30d
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Latest readings on a public mesh need no credentials (publish it with
### "is_public": true first). Timeseries and quality still need a key.
GET {{readingsUrl}}/readings/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}/latest

### Radio links heard on a mesh, longest first. since is 24h, 7d (default) or 30d.
GET {{registryUrl}}/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}/links?since=7d
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Traceroutes heard on a mesh, newest first; pass the last heard_at as `before` for more.
GET {{registryUrl}}/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}/traceroutes?limit=20
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Mesh traffic stats: totals, packets by portnum, busiest nodes, gateways, hops, signal.
### range is 1h, 24h (default), 7d or 30d; no key needed on a public mesh.
GET {{readingsUrl}}/readings/meshes/{{createMesh.response.body.$.id}}/stats?range=24h
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### One device's packets by portnum and over time
GET {{readingsUrl}}/readings/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}/traffic?range=7d
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Timeseries for a device/measurement
GET {{readingsUrl}}/readings/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}?channel=device_metrics.battery_level&start=-1h&agg=mean
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Latest reading for one measurement
GET {{readingsUrl}}/readings/devices/{{createNode.response.body.$.uuid}}/latest?payload_kind=telemetry:device_metrics&lookback=-30d
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### readings has no device, channel or catalog listing - what *exists* is
### registry's job; see the registry sections above for GET /devices,
### /devices/{id}/measurements and /measurements.

### Device listing, removed -> 404
GET {{readingsUrl}}/readings/nodes?lookback=-30d
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}

### Catalog read-through, removed -> 404
GET {{readingsUrl}}/readings/measurements
Authorization: Bearer {{signup.response.body.$.api_key}}
